1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,300
Welcome to Session two.

2
00:00:02,300 --> 00:00:05,500
Let me say at the start what this session is not.

3
00:00:05,500 --> 00:00:07,850
It is not a Python course.

4
00:00:07,850 --> 00:00:16,800
You have had that, in DA seven zero six six, and I am going to assume you can write a loop, call a function and read an error message without panic.

5
00:00:16,800 --> 00:00:19,700
There is almost no new syntax today.

6
00:00:19,700 --> 00:00:22,825
What this session is about is how you work.

7
00:00:22,825 --> 00:00:29,600
The difference between somebody who knows Python and somebody who can be trusted with an analysis is not syntax.

8
00:00:29,600 --> 00:00:41,700
It is a set of habits: choosing the right object for your data, looking at data before computing on it, reaching for a library instead of writing it yourself, and having a routine for when things go wrong.

9
00:00:41,700 --> 00:00:53,000
And there is one sentence that runs through the whole session, which I will repeat several times and which I would like you to leave with: code that runs without an error does not mean the analysis is correct.

10
00:00:53,000 --> 00:00:56,775
That is the real hazard in computational biology.

11
00:00:56,775 --> 00:01:01,400
A program that crashes is a nuisance; you will fix it in ten minutes.

12
00:01:01,400 --> 00:01:08,325
A program that runs cleanly and gives you a number that is wrong can go into a thesis, and then into a paper.

13
00:01:08,325 --> 00:01:16,225
We will use one real dataset all morning, a mouse liver RNA-seq experiment, and one gene family, the human globins.

14
00:01:16,225 --> 00:01:21,275
And in Block two I will do some live coding and make three mistakes on purpose.

15
00:01:21,275 --> 00:01:28,875
A practical note: the notebooks run on Colab, either from VS Code or in the browser, and the setup page tells you how.

16
00:01:28,875 --> 00:01:32,025
Do that before the lab rather than during it.

17
00:01:32,025 --> 00:01:37,925
Everything in this lecture is also written out in full on the Session two page of the course book.

18
00:01:37,925 --> 00:01:46,150
The page is the reference and this is the walk through it, so whenever a number or a name goes past too quickly, that is where to look it up.

19
00:01:46,150 --> 00:01:50,175
I will say which part of the page we are in as each block starts.

20
00:01:50,775 --> 00:01:54,075
Four ideas, and they are the structure of the morning.

21
00:01:54,075 --> 00:01:56,050
One: represent.

22
00:01:56,050 --> 00:01:58,975
Choose the right object for your data.

23
00:01:58,975 --> 00:02:06,725
Python will let you put numbers in almost anything, and the choice decides which operations you get and which mistakes are possible.

24
00:02:06,725 --> 00:02:08,600
Two: explore.

25
00:02:08,600 --> 00:02:11,550
Look at the data before you compute on it.

26
00:02:11,550 --> 00:02:14,925
Summaries and plots first, tests afterwards.

27
00:02:14,925 --> 00:02:19,075
This is the habit that catches the most errors for the least effort.

28
00:02:19,075 --> 00:02:21,425
Three: use what exists.

29
00:02:21,425 --> 00:02:28,925
Standard questions have standard functions, written and tested by people who thought harder about the edge cases than you will.

30
00:02:28,925 --> 00:02:31,075
Four: get unstuck.

31
00:02:31,075 --> 00:02:35,325
When something does not work, follow a routine instead of guessing.

32
00:02:35,325 --> 00:02:40,100
That is Block three, and it is the one that gets the live coding in Block two.

33
00:02:40,100 --> 00:02:44,250
One real dataset and one gene family carry all four.

34
00:02:44,250 --> 00:02:48,825
The dataset is mouse liver RNA-seq from Becares and colleagues.

35
00:02:48,825 --> 00:02:54,175
The gene family is the human globins, which we have been following since Session one.

36
00:02:54,175 --> 00:02:59,600
Come back to this slide at the end, and check that you can say what each of the four means.

37
00:03:00,200 --> 00:03:01,675
Block one.

38
00:03:01,675 --> 00:03:04,025
Represent and explore.

39
00:03:04,025 --> 00:03:11,100
On the page, this block is the sections "Scientific Python objects" and "Exploring a biological dataset".

40
00:03:11,100 --> 00:03:12,775
Two halves.

41
00:03:12,775 --> 00:03:22,350
First, objects: what it means to choose the right one, from a plain list up to a Biopython record and a PyTorch model, and how to write a class of your own.

42
00:03:22,350 --> 00:03:26,875
Then a real dataset, and the first things to type when you meet one.

43
00:03:27,475 --> 00:03:33,650
Here is the same information, three transcript lengths, held in increasingly specialised objects.

44
00:03:33,650 --> 00:03:35,875
A plain Python list.

45
00:03:35,875 --> 00:03:40,450
It holds anything, in order, and it knows nothing about what it holds.

46
00:03:40,450 --> 00:03:42,500
A NumPy array.

47
00:03:42,500 --> 00:03:51,900
The same three numbers, but now they are numbers: arithmetic works element-wise, and operations on whole arrays are fast because they run in compiled code.

48
00:03:51,900 --> 00:03:54,825
A pandas Series or DataFrame.

49
00:03:54,825 --> 00:03:59,850
Now the numbers have labels, and you can select, group and join on those labels.

50
00:03:59,850 --> 00:04:12,475
And then the domain-specific ones: a Biopython Seq, which knows that it is a sequence and that sequences can be complemented and translated; a PyTorch tensor, which remembers how it was computed.

51
00:04:12,475 --> 00:04:17,125
The principle to take away is not that specialised is always better.

52
00:04:17,125 --> 00:04:25,075
It is that each object gives you the operations that make sense for its kind of data, and refuses or mangles the ones that do not.

53
00:04:25,075 --> 00:04:28,950
Choosing the object is choosing which mistakes are possible.

54
00:04:29,550 --> 00:04:35,625
Here is that abstract point as a demonstration, and it is the first example of today's theme.

55
00:04:35,625 --> 00:04:39,025
Take a list of numbers, and multiply it by two.

56
00:04:39,025 --> 00:04:40,700
What do you get?

57
00:04:40,700 --> 00:04:43,150
You get a list twice as long.

58
00:04:43,150 --> 00:04:49,700
Python's list multiplication repeats the list: the numbers are all still there, each appearing twice.

59
00:04:49,700 --> 00:04:54,275
Now take a NumPy array of the same numbers and multiply it by two.

60
00:04:54,275 --> 00:04:57,500
You get the same three numbers, each doubled.

61
00:04:57,500 --> 00:05:01,700
Two completely different results from code that looks identical.

62
00:05:01,700 --> 00:05:05,775
And here is the important part: neither of them is an error.

63
00:05:05,775 --> 00:05:07,350
Both run.

64
00:05:07,350 --> 00:05:09,450
Both produce something.

65
00:05:09,450 --> 00:05:21,300
If your next line takes the mean, one of those answers is the mean you wanted and the other is the same mean computed from twice as many copies, which happens to be identical, so you would never notice.

66
00:05:21,300 --> 00:05:27,750
That is the shape of the dangerous mistake, and we will meet two more serious versions of it in the live coding.

67
00:05:28,350 --> 00:05:34,575
One object deserves its own slide, because everything from Session seven onwards depends on it.

68
00:05:34,575 --> 00:05:42,700
A PyTorch tensor is like a NumPy array, with one addition: if you ask it to, it records every operation performed on it.

69
00:05:42,700 --> 00:05:51,225
Then, when you call backward on a result, it replays those operations in reverse and gives you the derivative with respect to every input.

70
00:05:51,225 --> 00:05:54,525
Let us check it against something you did by hand.

71
00:05:54,525 --> 00:06:02,475
In Session one, the practice problem was f equals three x nought squared, plus two x nought x one, at the point one, two.

72
00:06:02,475 --> 00:06:13,525
You computed the partial derivatives: d f by d x nought is six x nought plus two x one, which is ten, and d f by d x one is two x nought, which is two.

73
00:06:13,525 --> 00:06:16,125
So the gradient is ten, two.

74
00:06:16,125 --> 00:06:21,550
Type the same function with PyTorch tensors, call backward, and print the gradients.

75
00:06:21,550 --> 00:06:23,375
Ten and two.

76
00:06:23,375 --> 00:06:29,725
That is the whole of automatic differentiation, which is Session one's chain rule applied mechanically.

77
00:06:29,725 --> 00:06:39,300
And it is the reason that from Session seven onwards you never differentiate anything by hand: you write the forward computation, and the derivative comes for free.

78
00:06:39,300 --> 00:06:49,325
It is worth seeing this once on a function you can check, so that when it is computing gradients for a network with a hundred thousand weights you know exactly what it is doing.

79
00:06:49,925 --> 00:06:52,800
And one object that knows biology.

80
00:06:52,800 --> 00:06:57,600
A Biopython Seq is a string that knows it is a biological sequence.

81
00:06:57,600 --> 00:07:02,375
It can give you the reverse complement without you writing a complement table.

82
00:07:02,375 --> 00:07:08,975
It can translate, applying the genetic code, including non-standard codon tables if you ask.

83
00:07:08,975 --> 00:07:11,875
It knows about ambiguity codes.

84
00:07:11,875 --> 00:07:16,350
On the slide: the first ten codons of HBB, translated.

85
00:07:16,350 --> 00:07:29,050
And a SeqRecord wraps a Seq with everything else a database record carries: an identifier, a description, and features, which are the annotations saying this stretch is the coding sequence, this one is an exon.

86
00:07:29,050 --> 00:07:37,825
In object terms, which matters for the next few slides: record dot seq and record dot features are attributes, the data the object holds.

87
00:07:37,825 --> 00:07:43,675
And record dot seq dot translate is a method, a function that belongs to the object.

88
00:07:43,675 --> 00:07:47,750
The next three slides show how to write such a class yourself.

89
00:07:48,350 --> 00:07:52,925
A question for you, and I would like you to commit to an answer before I show you.

90
00:07:52,925 --> 00:08:00,700
We have the HBB sequence, and hbb dot translate gives us the peptide M V H L T P E E K S.

91
00:08:00,700 --> 00:08:05,450
Now what does hbb, square bracket one colon, dot translate give?

92
00:08:05,450 --> 00:08:09,425
That is, drop the first base, and translate what is left.

93
00:08:09,425 --> 00:08:11,225
Four options.

94
00:08:11,225 --> 00:08:15,400
A: the same peptide, M V H L T P E E K S.

95
00:08:15,400 --> 00:08:17,175
B: an error.

96
00:08:17,175 --> 00:08:19,525
C: a different peptide.

97
00:08:19,525 --> 00:08:21,575
D: nothing at all.

98
00:08:21,575 --> 00:08:24,750
Think about it properly before continuing.

99
00:08:24,750 --> 00:08:29,700
What does dropping one base do to how the sequence is divided into codons?

100
00:08:29,700 --> 00:08:32,800
Pause here if you are watching this on your own.

101
00:08:33,400 --> 00:08:36,425
The answer is C: a different peptide.

102
00:08:36,425 --> 00:08:39,550
Dropping one base shifts the reading frame.

103
00:08:39,550 --> 00:08:48,100
Every codon boundary moves by one, so every codon is different, and the peptide that comes out has nothing to do with the real protein.

104
00:08:48,100 --> 00:08:53,525
In this case a stop codon appears partway through, which is typical of a wrong frame.

105
00:08:53,525 --> 00:08:56,500
Now, the crucial thing: there is no error.

106
00:08:56,500 --> 00:09:03,550
Biopython emits a warning, because the length is no longer a multiple of three, and then translates it anyway.

107
00:09:03,550 --> 00:09:08,225
You get a string of amino acids that looks exactly like a protein.

108
00:09:08,225 --> 00:09:16,650
And that is a mistake of the third kind, which we will name properly in Block two: biologically nonsensical, but perfectly valid Python.

109
00:09:16,650 --> 00:09:20,325
Nothing in the software knows which reading frame you meant.

110
00:09:20,325 --> 00:09:24,725
Only you know that, and only biology tells you the answer is wrong.

111
00:09:24,725 --> 00:09:29,575
If you had chosen B, an error, you were assuming the library would protect you.

112
00:09:29,575 --> 00:09:34,550
It will not, and that assumption is the thing I most want to dislodge this morning.

113
00:09:35,150 --> 00:09:39,150
Which raises the question: how do you translate correctly?

114
00:09:39,150 --> 00:09:41,700
Here is the shape of the problem.

115
00:09:41,700 --> 00:09:46,300
The HBB mRNA is six hundred and twenty-eight nucleotides.

116
00:09:46,300 --> 00:09:49,250
It does not begin with the start codon.

117
00:09:49,250 --> 00:09:56,550
It begins with a fifty-nucleotide untranslated region, and there is another untranslated region at the other end.

118
00:09:56,550 --> 00:10:01,475
Only the middle part, the coding sequence, is translated into protein.

119
00:10:01,475 --> 00:10:08,525
So translating the whole mRNA is wrong twice over: you start in the wrong place, and you run past the end.

120
00:10:08,525 --> 00:10:16,150
The record knows this, because GenBank records carry a CDS feature marking exactly where the coding sequence is.

121
00:10:16,150 --> 00:10:25,875
So the correct call extracts that feature first and translates it: cds dot extract of the record's sequence, dot translate, with to-stop set to true.

122
00:10:25,875 --> 00:10:30,675
That gives a hundred and forty-seven amino acids, which is a globin.

123
00:10:30,675 --> 00:10:39,000
Remember this slide, because in the live coding I am going to forget it on purpose, and get a six-hundred-and-fifty-four-residue globin.

124
00:10:39,600 --> 00:10:42,625
Now, how do you write an object of your own?

125
00:10:42,625 --> 00:10:48,500
Five minutes on classes, because every PyTorch model from Session eight onwards is one.

126
00:10:48,500 --> 00:10:50,800
A class is a blueprint.

127
00:10:50,800 --> 00:10:54,775
It says what objects of this kind hold and what they can do.

128
00:10:54,775 --> 00:10:59,125
The method called double-underscore init runs when you make an object.

129
00:10:59,125 --> 00:11:02,325
Its job is to store the data you pass in.

130
00:11:02,325 --> 00:11:08,725
So Protein, with a name and a sequence, creates an empty object and hands it to init, which stores them.

131
00:11:08,725 --> 00:11:12,600
The things it stores are attributes: the object's data.

132
00:11:12,600 --> 00:11:18,100
And the other functions in the class are methods: things the object can do with its data.

133
00:11:18,100 --> 00:11:21,675
Then self, which is the word that confuses people.

134
00:11:21,675 --> 00:11:24,275
Self is the object itself.

135
00:11:24,275 --> 00:11:30,175
When you write beta dot length, Python calls the length method with beta as its first argument.

136
00:11:30,175 --> 00:11:35,500
So beta dot length is exactly the same call as Protein dot length of beta.

137
00:11:35,500 --> 00:11:40,025
Self is just how the method refers to whichever object it was called on.

138
00:11:40,025 --> 00:11:41,375
Why bother?

139
00:11:41,375 --> 00:11:47,775
Because a hundred Protein objects share one copy of the methods while each has its own name and sequence.

140
00:11:47,775 --> 00:11:52,000
The behaviour is defined once; the data is per object.

141
00:11:52,000 --> 00:11:57,975
And then the special methods, the ones with two underscores on each side, which Python calls for you.

142
00:11:57,975 --> 00:12:02,875
Define double-underscore len and the built-in len works on your object.

143
00:12:02,875 --> 00:12:07,300
Define double-underscore repr and you control what print shows.

144
00:12:07,300 --> 00:12:14,675
Define double-underscore call and your object can be called like a function, which is the one that matters in two slides' time.

145
00:12:15,275 --> 00:12:19,375
Inheritance, briefly, with one bug that catches everyone.

146
00:12:19,375 --> 00:12:24,325
If an Enzyme is a kind of Protein, you do not rewrite the Protein code.

147
00:12:24,325 --> 00:12:30,525
You declare that Enzyme inherits from Protein, and it gets every method Protein has, for free.

148
00:12:30,525 --> 00:12:36,250
Then you add what is new: an EC number, say, and whatever methods only enzymes need.

149
00:12:36,250 --> 00:12:38,325
And here is the bug.

150
00:12:38,325 --> 00:12:45,725
In Enzyme's init, you must call super dot init first, which runs Protein's init and stores the name and the sequence.

151
00:12:45,725 --> 00:12:48,700
Forget that line, and watch what happens.

152
00:12:48,700 --> 00:12:52,000
The object is created with no error at all.

153
00:12:52,000 --> 00:12:53,700
It looks fine.

154
00:12:53,700 --> 00:13:02,500
Then, some time later, you call weight, which looks for self dot sequence, and you get an AttributeError saying the object has no attribute sequence.

155
00:13:02,500 --> 00:13:09,075
So the error appears in a method that is completely correct, a long way from the line that is actually wrong.

156
00:13:09,075 --> 00:13:16,875
That is a mistake of the first kind, a genuine Python error, but one where the message points at the symptom rather than the cause.

157
00:13:16,875 --> 00:13:19,625
Which is the subject of Block three.

158
00:13:20,225 --> 00:13:23,850
And here is why I spent five minutes on classes.

159
00:13:23,850 --> 00:13:28,750
Every PyTorch model is a class that inherits from nn dot Module.

160
00:13:28,750 --> 00:13:30,225
Two parts.

161
00:13:30,225 --> 00:13:35,175
In init, you create the layers and store them as attributes: what the model has.

162
00:13:35,175 --> 00:13:42,500
In forward, you take an input tensor and return an output tensor, by calling those layers in order: what the model does.

163
00:13:42,500 --> 00:13:46,700
And then you use it by calling the object itself, model of x.

164
00:13:46,700 --> 00:13:54,025
Which works because nn dot Module defines double-underscore call: it does some bookkeeping and then calls your forward.

165
00:13:54,025 --> 00:14:02,125
So model of x and model dot forward of x are nearly the same thing, and you should always write the first, because the bookkeeping matters.

166
00:14:02,125 --> 00:14:08,525
One more consequence of storing the layers as attributes, which is the bit that makes training possible at all.

167
00:14:08,525 --> 00:14:15,275
Because the layers are attributes, nn dot Module can walk through the object and find every weight inside it.

168
00:14:15,275 --> 00:14:21,800
That is what model dot parameters returns, and it is what you hand to the optimiser in Session nine.

169
00:14:21,800 --> 00:14:26,750
For the small model on this slide, four parameters: three weights and a bias.

170
00:14:26,750 --> 00:14:31,000
So: the class machinery you have just learned is not an aside.

171
00:14:31,000 --> 00:14:35,900
It is the interface you will use for every model in the second half of this course.

172
00:14:36,500 --> 00:14:40,600
Now a real dataset, and the habits that go with meeting one.

173
00:14:40,600 --> 00:14:45,900
This is from Becares and colleagues, published in Cell Reports in twenty nineteen.

174
00:14:45,900 --> 00:14:54,575
Mice with a mutation in the LXR-alpha protein, called S one nine six A, compared with wild-type mice, both on a high-fat diet.

175
00:14:54,575 --> 00:14:57,475
Liver tissue, RNA sequenced.

176
00:14:57,475 --> 00:14:59,600
Three mice per group.

177
00:14:59,600 --> 00:15:14,500
The data has already been analysed with DESeq2, which is the standard tool for this, so what we have is one row per gene with the results: a base mean expression level, a log two fold change between the groups, a p-value and an adjusted p-value.

178
00:15:14,500 --> 00:15:17,150
Three thousand and one genes.

179
00:15:17,150 --> 00:15:22,025
And then the four things you should type whenever you meet a new table, in this order.

180
00:15:22,025 --> 00:15:25,100
df dot shape, to see how big it is.

181
00:15:25,100 --> 00:15:31,025
df dot columns, to see what the columns are actually called, which is rarely what you assumed.

182
00:15:31,025 --> 00:15:35,450
df dot head, to look at the first few rows with your own eyes.

183
00:15:35,450 --> 00:15:42,600
And df dot describe, which gives you the count, mean, minimum, maximum and quartiles of every numeric column.

184
00:15:42,600 --> 00:15:45,150
Four commands, ten seconds.

185
00:15:45,150 --> 00:15:47,775
The next slide is what they tell you.

186
00:15:48,375 --> 00:15:53,725
Here is what describe told us, and it is a good illustration of why you type it.

187
00:15:53,725 --> 00:15:57,600
The largest p-value in this file is nought point one two.

188
00:15:57,600 --> 00:16:00,000
Stop and think about that.

189
00:16:00,000 --> 00:16:06,125
A genome-wide experiment measures every gene, and most genes are not differentially expressed.

190
00:16:06,125 --> 00:16:11,675
So p-values should run all the way up to one, and in fact most of them should be large.

191
00:16:11,675 --> 00:16:16,175
A maximum of nought point one two means this is not all the genes.

192
00:16:16,175 --> 00:16:22,300
It is a pre-filtered list: somebody has already removed everything that was clearly not changing.

193
00:16:22,300 --> 00:16:27,925
The largest adjusted p-value is nought point five zero, which tells the same story.

194
00:16:27,925 --> 00:16:31,575
This matters because of what you can and cannot claim.

195
00:16:31,575 --> 00:16:42,250
Any statement of the form "in this experiment, x per cent of genes were differentially expressed" would be wrong, because the denominator is not the genome, it is a filtered subset.

196
00:16:42,250 --> 00:16:51,775
Any statistical procedure that assumes you have the full distribution of p-values, which includes several multiple-testing corrections, would also be wrong.

197
00:16:51,775 --> 00:16:54,575
And none of that is visible in the code.

198
00:16:54,575 --> 00:16:58,450
It is visible in describe, in ten seconds, if you look.

199
00:16:58,450 --> 00:17:02,200
That is the whole argument for exploring before computing.

200
00:17:02,800 --> 00:17:08,125
A practical slide on pandas, with the one piece of syntax that catches everyone.

201
00:17:08,125 --> 00:17:17,050
To select rows, you put a condition in square brackets: df, square bracket, df, square bracket, padj, close, less than nought point zero five.

202
00:17:17,050 --> 00:17:22,750
The inner part produces a column of True and False, and the outer part keeps the True rows.

203
00:17:22,750 --> 00:17:26,700
To combine conditions, you use the symbols, not the words.

204
00:17:26,700 --> 00:17:29,950
Ampersand for and, vertical bar for or.

205
00:17:29,950 --> 00:17:37,050
And you must put parentheses around each condition, because those symbols bind more tightly than the comparison does.

206
00:17:37,050 --> 00:17:47,525
So: df, with the condition, padj less than nought point zero five, in parentheses, ampersand, the absolute log two fold change greater than one, in parentheses.

207
00:17:47,525 --> 00:17:53,925
If you use the English word and, you will get an error saying the truth value of a Series is ambiguous.

208
00:17:53,925 --> 00:18:03,125
That message is confusing the first time, and it means exactly this: the word and needs a single True or False, and you gave it three thousand of them.

209
00:18:03,125 --> 00:18:08,000
The ampersand combines them element by element, which is what you wanted.

210
00:18:08,600 --> 00:18:13,100
And then plots, because a number is a summary and a picture is the data.

211
00:18:13,100 --> 00:18:15,125
Three on this slide.

212
00:18:15,125 --> 00:18:20,875
A histogram, for one variable at a time: what is the distribution of fold changes?

213
00:18:20,875 --> 00:18:27,800
A scatter plot on a logarithmic axis, for two variables: is fold change related to expression level?

214
00:18:27,800 --> 00:18:37,100
And a volcano plot, which is fold change against significance, coloured by whether a gene passes the threshold, drawn with seaborn in about one line.

215
00:18:37,100 --> 00:18:40,500
Now look at the first two panels and find the gap.

216
00:18:40,500 --> 00:18:46,300
There is an empty band around zero: very few genes with a fold change close to nothing.

217
00:18:46,300 --> 00:18:52,475
In a complete experiment, that is where the tallest bar would be, because most genes do not change.

218
00:18:52,475 --> 00:18:56,525
The gap is the pre-filtering again, visible a second way.

219
00:18:56,525 --> 00:19:06,275
And that is the argument for plotting: the same fact appeared in describe as a maximum p-value of nought point one two, and in the histogram as a hole in the middle.

220
00:19:06,275 --> 00:19:10,650
One of those two will catch your eye, and you do not know in advance which.

